# WebGL: GC and Memory

WebGL runs in a **WASM linear memory heap**. It can grow but never shrinks back to the browser, and hitting the configured maximum is a hard crash (`Out of memory` in the tab, no recovery). Memory discipline is a correctness requirement here, not just a perf nicety.

## GC behavior on WebGL

- The GC on WebGL runs **only between frames**, not concurrently or incrementally mid-frame the way desktop incremental GC can. A large garbage backlog means a visible hitch when collection finally runs.
- Allocation-heavy frames therefore translate directly into stutter. The fix is not "tune the GC" — it is **don't allocate** (see allocation-discipline and update-loop-hygiene rules).

## Heap growth is a ratchet

- Every allocation spike (loading burst, huge temporary buffer, giant string) permanently grows the WASM heap to fit the peak.
- **Pre-allocate at load time**: pools, buffers, native containers sized to peak gameplay demand while the loading screen is up. Steady-state gameplay should allocate ~zero.
- Avoid patterns that need "double memory" transiently: loading a new copy of data before releasing the old one. Release first, or stream in chunks.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — builds a giant temporary string (heap spike, permanent growth)
string json = JsonConvert.SerializeObject(fullHistory);

// ✅ GOOD — bounded state; big payloads streamed / chunked / avoided client-side
```

## Asset memory

- Texture and audio memory dominate. Respect max sizes and compression (see build-size rule); an uncompressed 2048² RGBA texture is 16 MB of heap by itself.
- Unload what you're done with: `Resources.UnloadAsset` / `AssetBundle.Unload(true)` at **transition points** (screen changes, match end).
- Do **not** call `Resources.UnloadUnusedAssets()` mid-gameplay — it's a multi-frame stall. Schedule it behind loading/transition screens only.

## Watch string churn

Strings are the most common invisible allocator: UI text, log messages, JSON. Rebuild UI strings only on change; keep payload parsing to one pass; never build debug strings that aren't shipped (use `[Conditional]` — see the logging-discipline rule).

## Agent checklist

- [ ] Peak-demand buffers/pools allocated during loading, not mid-gameplay
- [ ] No transient double-memory loading patterns
- [ ] `UnloadUnusedAssets` only at transition points, never during play
- [ ] No unbounded growth: histories, logs, and caches have caps
